== Products ==
From twenty-five pharmaceutical production operations in India and Zydus Cadila develops and manufactures an extensive range of pharmaceuticals as well as diagnostics, [[Herbal medicine|herbal]] products, skincare products and other OTC products. Starting from late 2015, having concluded a voluntary license agreement with [[Gilead Sciences|Gilead]], the company also produces the generics for [[hepatitis C]] treatment (i.e. [[sofosbuvir]], distributed under the brand name SoviHep).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://hep-c-news.com/hepatitis-c-treatment-in-india-for-less-than-1000/|title=Hepatitis C treatment in India for less than $1000|date=11 July 2016|website=hep-c-news.com|publisher=Hep C News|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160916124038/http://hep-c-news.com/blog/2016/07/11/hepatitis-c-treatment-in-india-for-less-than-1000/|archive-date=16 September 2016|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The company makes [[active pharmaceutical ingredient]]s at three sites in India, [[Ankleshwar]] plants, [[Vadodara]] plant, and [[Patalganga]] plant.

==COVID-19 vaccine development==
[[File:The Prime Minister, Shri Narendra Modi visits the Zydus Biotech Park, in Ahmedabad, Gujarat on November 28, 2020 (7).jpg|thumb|Prime Minister [[Narendra Modi]] during his visit to the Zydus Biotech Park in Ahmedabad in 2020.]]
In July 2020, the company got permission to conduct human trials of the developmental [[COVID-19 vaccine]] named [[ZyCoV-D]], from the [[Drugs Controller General of India]] (DCGI), [[Government of India]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Potential COVID-19 vaccine by Zydus Cadila gets DCGI nod for human clinical trials |url=https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/health/potential-covid-19-vaccine-by-zydus-cadila-gets-dcgi-nod-for-human-clinical-trials/article31977556.ece |work=The Hindu |date=3 July 2020 |language=en-IN}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Zydus Cadila begins human clinical trials of COVID-19 vaccine candidate - ET HealthWorld |url=https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/pharma/zydus-cadila-begins-human-clinical-trials-of-covid-19-vaccine-candidate/76975377 |work=ETHealthworld.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Zydus Cadila's COVID-19 Vaccine Candidate Found Safe in Early-Stage Human Trial |url=https://www.nytimes.com/reuters/2020/08/05/world/asia/05reuters-health-coronavirus-zydus-vaccine.html |work=The New York Times |date=5 August 2020}}</ref> On 20 August 2021, DCGI granted emergency use approval of ZyCoV-D.<ref>{{cite news |title=India gives emergency approval for world's first COVID-19 DNA vaccine |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/healthcare-pharmaceuticals/india-approves-zydus-cadilas-covid-19-vaccine-emergency-use-2021-08-20/ |work=Reuters |date=20 August 2021 }}</ref>

Cadila is also among the several Indian pharma companies that received the licensing agreements from the [[Gilead Sciences]] to produce ''[[remdesivir]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Umarji |first1=Vinay |title=Gilead finds fourth partner for manufacture of Remdesivir in Zydus Cadila |url=https://www.business-standard.com/article/companies/gilead-finds-fourth-partner-for-manufacture-of-remdesivir-in-zydus-cadila-120061201687_1.html |work=Business Standard India |date=12 June 2020}}</ref>

== Corporate control ==
Zydus Cadila's major shareholder remains the Patel family. [[Pankaj Patel]] (born 1953), son of the founder, is the chairman of the company. In 2004, Pankaj Patel was included in ''[[Forbes|Forbes'<nowiki/>]]'' annual list of India's richest people. As of 2020, ''Forbes'' estimates Patel's net worth at US$3.9 billion, making him India's 46th richest person.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Pankaj Patel|url=https://www.forbes.com/profile/pankaj-patel/|access-date=2020-06-15|website=Forbes}}</ref>

== Controversies ==
A ''[[Mint (newspaper)|Mint]]'' investigative report in 2021 claimed that Zydus Lifesciences supplied possibly bacteria-contaminated batches of [[remdesivir]] to hospitals in [[Uttar Pradesh]], [[Rajasthan]], [[Maharashtra]], [[Gujarat]] and [[Bihar]], allegedly making hundreds of already sick COVID-19 patients even sicker.{{medical citation needed|date=May 2024}} Zydus was manufacturing the remdesivir in their Gujarat factory under license from United States' [[Gilead Sciences]]. In May 2021, when state procurement agency Rajasthan Medical Services Corporation sought an explanation, Zydus stated that they had not seen similar adverse reactions to their remdesivir elsewhere and denied all contamination allegations.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-12-23 |title=The dangerous failure to stop tainted remdesivir |url=https://www.livemint.com/science/health/the-dangerous-failure-to-stop-tainted-remdesivir-11640197634967.html |access-date=2023-08-01 |website=mint |language=en}}</ref>{{Primary source inline|dat=May 2024|date=May 2024}}

In December 2016, Zydus Discovery DMCC, a research subsidiary Zydus Lifesciences, was cited by the [[Food and Drug Administration | US FDA]] for deliberately misbranding their flagship product, [[Saroglitazar]]—the drug used to treat diabetic dyslipidemia in India. In a 21 December 2016 letter to the company, the US FDA asked it to stop using broad statements, such as the "world's first" and to stop suggesting that the drug is approved throughout the world, including in the United States, when that is not true.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Dandekar |first=Vikas |date=2016-12-28 |title=Cadila Healthcare misbranded Saroglitazar, says US FDA |work=The Economic Times |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/healthcare/biotech/pharmaceuticals/cadila-healthcare-misbranded-saroglitazar-says-us-fda/articleshow/56214730.cms |access-date=2023-08-01 |issn=0013-0389}}
</ref>

